Linux Special Cases (Mullvad, Ventoy)
=====================================
Some tools have no install command that works through a plain
InstallCommand on Linux, either because the package isn't in any
distro's repo, or because it's only in a repo that has to be added
first. These are special-cased in installer.Install() before the
generic Item.CommandFor() path, the same way PHP is special-cased on
Windows (see WINDOWS_PHP).


Mullvad VPN (installer/linux_mullvad.go)
----------------------------------------
Mullvad has no package in the standard Debian/Ubuntu or Fedora repos -
only through Mullvad's own self-hosted repo. It IS available in
Arch's official "extra" repo though, so that case doesn't need any
special handling.

  Pacman:      uses the normal InstallCommand in catalog/data.go,
               nothing special.
  Apt:         InstallMullvadApt() downloads Mullvad's signing key to
               /usr/share/keyrings/mullvad-keyring.asc, writes
               /etc/apt/sources.list.d/mullvad.list using the
               distro's VERSION_CODENAME (read from /etc/os-release)
               and dpkg's architecture, then runs apt-get update +
               apt-get install.
  Dnf:         InstallMullvadDnf() adds Mullvad's .repo file via
               "dnf config-manager addrepo --from-repofile=..."
               (dnf5 / Fedora 41+), falling back to the older
               "dnf config-manager --add-repo ..." syntax (dnf4 /
               Fedora 40 and earlier) if that fails, then runs
               dnf install.
  Zypper/Apk:  not supported - Mullvad doesn't provide a repo for
               openSUSE or Alpine, and there's no reasonable
               community package to fall back to. CommandFor()
               returns ok = false and the item is skipped normally.

installer.Install() intercepts item.ID == "mullvad" only for Apt and
Dnf; Pacman falls through to the generic path.


Ventoy (installer/linux_ventoy.go)
----------------------------------
Ventoy has no package in any standard Linux distro repository at
all - on Arch it's AUR-only ("ventoy-bin"), and Dumpware doesn't
shell out to an AUR helper. Everywhere on Linux it's a GitHub release
tarball only, so that's what InstallVentoyLinux() does:

  1. Queries the GitHub API for the latest release
     (api.github.com/repos/ventoy/Ventoy/releases/latest) and finds
     the asset ending in "-linux.tar.gz".
  2. Downloads it to a temp file.
  3. Extracts it to /opt/ventoy, guarding against tar-slip path
     traversal on every entry (same principle as windows_php.go's
     zip-slip protection).
  4. Prints the path to the extracted Ventoy2Disk.sh script, since
     actually writing to a USB device needs a device path from the
     user and shouldn't happen unattended.

installer.Install() intercepts item.ID == "ventoy" for every Linux
package manager, including UnknownPM - the GitHub-tarball install
doesn't need a package manager at all.

Ventoy also has no native macOS installer upstream (confirmed - not
even a community Homebrew cask exists), so it stays Windows-only
outside of Linux.


Plain Omissions (no special-casing needed)
-------------------------------------------
Not every gap needs a linux_*.go file. When a distro simply has no
package to install and there's no reasonable repo to add on the fly,
the right fix is just leaving that PackageManager out of the Item's
Linux map in catalog/data.go - CommandFor() returns ok = false and
the wizard prints installer.noCommand and skips it, same as any
zero-valued platform. These are documented here so the next person
doesn't "fix" the gap by inventing a package name that doesn't exist.

  Visual Studio Code (item "vscode"):    no Linux entry at all.
                                          Microsoft only ships it via
                                          its own apt/yum repo (or
                                          Snap/Flatpak) - no distro's
                                          default repos carry it.

  Sublime Text (item "sublimetext"):     no Linux entry at all. Same
                                          situation as VS Code - needs
                                          Sublime's own apt/yum repo
                                          everywhere, and it's
                                          AUR-only on Arch
                                          ("sublime-text-4").

  Brave (item "brave"):                  no Linux entry at all. Needs
                                          Brave's own apt/yum repo on
                                          Debian/Ubuntu/Fedora, and is
                                          AUR-only on Arch
                                          ("brave-bin"). openSUSE has
                                          no official package either.

  Rust (item "rust"), Zypper only:       every other PM has a real
                                          Rust/Cargo package, but
                                          openSUSE's live in the OBS
                                          devel:languages:rust
                                          project, not Tumbleweed's
                                          default repos - a plain
                                          "zypper install rust cargo"
                                          fails without adding that
                                          repo first.

None of these are AUR-solvable the way Ventoy or Sublime's Arch case
might tempt you to think - Dumpware doesn't shell out to an AUR
helper, per the Ventoy precedent above.


Shared helpers (installer/util.go)
----------------------------------
downloadFile(url, destPath) and runCmd(name, args...) are small
helpers factored out for reuse across windows_php.go,
linux_mullvad.go and linux_ventoy.go, since all three do some
combination of "download a file" and "run a command with
stdout/stderr/stdin wired to the terminal".
